let's move un_fs and to_fs into RdKw/I18N/CodeSets.pm (still in the same repo, but create a 'codesets' directory). this will implement `-C ...` in most commands we have in ../engine/ .

the idea is to have a process global CS object, retrievable with CodeSets->locale (and settable via CodeSets->locale( ... ))

```
# diag is a pseudo-category, either 'ascii' or 'unicode'.
#   diag defaults to 'unicode' but is automatically downgraded
#   to 'ascii' when Encode::find_encoding( stderr ) is the
#   canonically also ascii.
# console is short for stdin/stdout/stderr
#   overrides all previous settings, though can be individually overridden.
# console_in is short for stdin
#   the default "locale" is a pseudo-encoding. it is short for "console_in" for these.
# console_out is short for stdout/stderr
#   "locale" is short for "console_out".
# argv is for @ARGV
#   "locale" is just itself
# env is for %ENV
#   "locale" for itself as well
# filesys is for syscall args/retvals of file operations
#   "locale" is short for "locale_fs"
#
# all of these can be get/set with $cs->$name, with the
# sole exception of diag, which is get/set via $cs->diag_mode.
CodeSets->new(
    diag => 'ascii',
    console => 'locale',
    argv => 'UTF-8',
    env => 'UTF-8',
    filesys => 'UTF-8',
);

# Omitted means "UTF-8"
Getopt::Long::GetOptions( 'C=s@' => \my @C );
$cs = CodeSets->parse(@C);  # -C diag:ascii -C console:locale -C argv:UTF-8 -C filesys
$cs = eval { CodeSets->parse(@C); } or die "E: codeset error: $@";   # re-throwing error in a CLI
CodeSets->locale($cs);

# inherit stdout from argv (for siteconf -l)
$cs->stdout( $cs->argv ) if !defined $cs->stdout;

# In a method elsewhere...
$cs = CodeSets->locale;
$cs->initstd;   # sets standard files (all of them default to their locales defined by Encode::Locale)
$cs->initstd(
   stdout => Encode::FB_CROAK(),   # sets $PerlIO::encoding::fallback
   stderr => Encode::FB_PERLQQ(),
);

$cs->diag_mode();  # shows 'unicode' or 'ascii' (this can be individually set to unicode, even if stderr encoding is UTF-8! this variable is not glued to stderr whatsoever. it's a general-purpose diagnostics method that happens to be usually meant for stderr.)
$cs->diag_name("a.png");  # safe characters get their usual `...' quoting
$cs->diag_name("\x89PNG\r\n\x1a\n");  # unsafe ASCII always becomes QQ quoted (Dumper Useqq)
use utf8; $cs->diag_name("炒饭.png");  # also quote as \x{...} unless we are under diag:unicode
use utf8; $cs->diag_name("炒饭\x{a0}pic.png\r\n");  # becomes "炒饭\x{a0}.png\r\n" under diag:unicode -- we use double quotes, but only manually escape dangerous characters (the same class Python escapes, /[\p{C}\p{Z}]/ (with ASCII space being the sole exception)
die $cs->diag("cannot open file %q: %s\n", $file, "$!");  # %q calls diag_name, %s interpolates directly.

$cs->un_argv(\@ARGV);  # die with something more helpful: e.g. cannot decode argv 3: $@  (error message from Encode::decode)
                       # when no arguments passed, mutate @ARGV  (future args may be added, so leave those alone)
$cs->to_argv(\@ARGV);  # similarly to encode to argv.

open $cs->to_fs( $file );
$cs->un_fs( getcwd() );

# refer to the syntax in ~/my/lib/perl5/IPC-Unix, minus their return values which are VOID here. ask me if i am contradicting myself. :c
$cs->getenv(qw( ONE TWO THREE ));  # get by keys. in scalar context, return the first. undef if not exists
$cs->setenv( KEY => "VALUE", ... ); # set keys; return nothing
$cs->unsetenv(qw( ONE TWO THREE ));  # clear by names; return nothing
```

